      "problem": "Find the exact solution of the equation $2^{x+1}=3.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we take $\\log_2$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+1} & =3 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡({2}^{x+1}) & ={log}_{2}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\log_b \\left( b^c \\right) = c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx+1 = \\log_2 \\left( 3 \\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & ={log}_{2}⁡(3) \\\\ x & ={log}_{2}⁡(3)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x= \\log_2 \\left( 3 \\right) - 1.$",
